    "['user-group [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'privilege group grp1', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'privilege group grp1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nprivilege group grp1\\n##\\n\\nExplanation: \\nIn Cisco IOS, the equivalent concept to Huawei\\'s `user-group` is a privilege group, which is used to assign task-based permissions to users. The command creates a new privilege group named \"grp1\" (similar to Huawei\\'s configuration). Note that Cisco uses a different permission model, so additional commands would be needed to fully replicate the Huawei functionality, but this is the direct equivalent of the `user-group` creation command.', 'trans_template': None}]]": 1,
    "['task-group [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'privilege exec level 1 write snmp', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'privilege exec level 1 write snmp', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nprivilege exec level 1 write snmp\\n##\\n\\nExplanation: \\nIn Huawei, `task-group tg1` with `task snmp write` defines a privilege group that allows SNMP write operations. The Cisco equivalent is to configure a privilege level (level 1 in this case) with SNMP write permissions using the `privilege exec` command. The exact permission granularity may vary slightly between platforms, but this is the closest functional equivalent.', 'trans_template': None}]]": 1,
